 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 TRANSLATOR'S NOTE}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sa0 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx567 \tql \tx7655 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \uc1 \u160 ?}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sa0 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx567 \tql \tx7655 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Students, teachers, artists, and members of the general public may download and distribute this text without permission and without charge. They may also edit or adapt it to suit their purposes. However, commercial publication is not permitted without the consent of Ian Johnston.\uc1 \u160 ?}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sa0 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx567 \tql \tx7655 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sa0 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx567 \tql \tx7655 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Note that in the following translation, the line numbers without brackets refer to the English text and those within square brackets refer to the Greek text. In the former, a partial indented line is normally combined in the reckoning with the short line immediately before it. Stage directions and footnotes have been supplied by the translator.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \qj \li0 \ri0 \sa0 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx567 \tql \tx7655 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sa200 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6400 \tql \tx7000 \tql \tx7260 \tql \tx7680 \tql \tx7700 \tql \tx7920 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 In this translation, possessives of words ending in }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 -s}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  are usually indicated in the common way (that is, by adding }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 -\uc1 \u8217 ?s}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  (e.g. }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Zeus}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  and }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Zeus\uc1 \u8217 ?s}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 ). This convention adds a syllable to the spoken word (the sound }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 -iz}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 ). Sometimes, for metrical reasons, this English text indicates such possession in an alternate manner, with a simple apostrophe. This form of the possessive does not add an extra syllable to the spoken name (e.g., }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Demos }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 and }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Demos\uc1 \u8217 ? }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 are both two-syllable words; whereas, }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Demos\uc1 \u8217 ?s }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 has three syllables). }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 The translator would like to acknowledge the valuable help provided by the notes in the editions of the play prepared by W. C. Green (1871), by W. W. Merry (1887), and by Alan Sommerstein (1981).}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \qc \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 INTRODUCTORY NOTE}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Aristophanes\uc1 \u8217 ? }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Knights}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  is a sharp, bawdy, and, in some places, grim satiric allegory on Athenian political life. While the targets of the satire are clear enough, the translator or editor is forced to make some decisions about the names of the characters, because specific names are given only to Demos (whose name means \uc1 \u8220 ?the people\uc1 \u8221 ?) and to the chorus of Knights.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 The main butt of the jokes is clearly Cleon, the popular demagogue of Athenian politics, but the character who represents him is called the Paphlagonian, and Cleon\uc1 \u8217 ?s name is mentioned only once in the play. The term }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Paphlagonian}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  refers not to an origin in Asia Minor but to his very aggressive rhetoric, since the name comes from the verb meaning }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 to bluster}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 . The Paphlagonian\uc1 \u8217 ?s main opponent, the Sausage Seller, does have a name (Agoracritus, meaning \uc1 \u8220 ?chosen by the marketplace\uc1 \u8221 ?), but that fact does not emerge until very late in the play. Hence, I have used the terms Paphlagonian and Sausage Seller to indicate these characters (some other editions of the play use the names Cleon and Agoracritus throughout).}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 The two slaves who open the play are not named specifically in the manuscripts, but traditionally they have been called Demosthenes and Nicias, after the two Athenian generals who were enemies of Cleon. I have retained these names because that seemed better than making up alternatives or calling them Slave A and Slave B.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 The term }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Knights}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  refers to an elite group of about a thousand cavalry in the Athenian military forces. Each Knight had to provide his own horse and would have expenses he would have to pay himself. However, membership was considered socially prestigious and would be drawn from the richer, more aristocratic Athenians, who tended to be hostile to the populist demagogue Cleon.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 At the time }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Knights}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  was first produced (424 BC), Athens and Sparta had been at war for about seven years. The previous year Athens had won an important victory at Pylos against the Spartans, capturing a number of prisoners and bringing them back to Athens. Cleon engineered things so that he received the major credit for this success. As a result, he acquired considerable popularity and was awarded a number of state honours. However, in the view of many Athenians he had, in effect, stolen the credit from Demosthenes. This point is frequently mentioned in the play.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 Knights}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033  was awarded first prize in the drama competition at the Lenaea festival in 424 BC.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \qc \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs22 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 DRAMATIS PERSONAE}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 DEMOSTHENES: a slave in the service of Demos}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \line }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 NICIAS: a slave in the service of Demos}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \line }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 SAUSAGE SELLER: a low-born Athenian street merchant}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \line }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 PAPHLAGONIAN: a slave in the service of Demos}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \line }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 DEMOS: an elderly Athenian citizen}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \line }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 CHORUS OF KNIGHTS.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af6 \loch \f6 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \par }\pard \plain \s23 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \sl240 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx7040 \tql \tx7700 \itap0 \fs21 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \